1. Row and column scaling at a cell that no perfect matching uses
   4 by 4 example; cells on no perfect matching: [(2, 0), (2, 1)]
   after    10 rounds the entry at (2, 0) is 0.0488836; 1/(2k) is 0.05
   after   100 rounds the entry at (2, 0) is 0.00505071; 1/(2k) is 0.005
   after  1000 rounds the entry at (2, 0) is 0.000501087; 1/(2k) is 0.0005
   after 10000 rounds the entry at (2, 0) is 5.00166e-05; 1/(2k) is 5e-05
   One example only.

2. Scaling as a lower bound on the permanent: cases where the computed bound exceeds the exact permanent
   200 random 0/1 matrices with a perfect matching per cell, seed 1
   n = 4: failures at 4, 16, 64, 1000 rounds: [0, 0, 0, 0]
   n = 6: failures at 4, 16, 64, 1000 rounds: [0, 0, 0, 0]
   n = 8: failures at 4, 16, 64, 1000 rounds: [0, 0, 0, 0]
   0 failures in 2400 cases. This is a sample, not a proof.

